  "result": "I added `click.KeyValue`. With `@click.option('--set', type=click.KeyValue())`, `--set a=1` comes through as `('a', '1')`. The full test suite passes, including the new tests.\n\nHow it parses:\n- It splits on the first `=`, so `a=b=c` gives `('a', 'b=c')`.\n- An empty value is allowed: `a=` gives `('a', '')`.\n- Input with no `=`, or with an empty key (`ab`, `=b`, `\"\"`, `=`), fails with exit code 2 and the message `'ab' is not a valid key=value pair.`\n- A value that is already a tuple is passed through unchanged, so tuple defaults work.\n- With `multiple=True`, `dict(pairs)` turns several `--set` options into a dict.\n\nChanges:\n- `src/click/types.py`: the new `KeyValue` class (help text shows it as `key=value`).\n- `src/click/__init__.py`: exports it as `click.KeyValue`.\n- `tests/test_types.py`: tests for valid input, invalid input and use with `multiple=True`.\n- `docs/api.md` and `docs/parameter-types.md`: added it to the documentation pages.\n- `CHANGES.md`: a new \"Unreleased\" entry, because the latest section there (8.5.0) is already released.\n\nNothing is committed yet.",
